lchains or SDKs.\\n   3. Restoring packages.\\n   4. Creating generated files.\\n   5. Running bootstrap or setup scripts.\\n   6. Initializing git submodules.\\n   7. Installing workspace/package manager dependencies.\\n   8. Building any required generated artifacts.\\n5. Do not start long-running processes such as development servers or watch tasks unless the README explicitly requires them as part of setup.\\n6. If any step requires user input, elevated privileges, secrets, credentials, or external configuration, stop and explain exactly what is needed before proceeding.\\n7. If multiple package managers or setup methods are available, use the one recommended by the project's documentation.\\n8. If the documentation contains conflicting instructions, prefer the instructions in the top-level \\\\`README.md\\\\` and explain any conflict.\\n9. When setup is complete, provide a concise summary including:\\n   1. The steps that were performed.\\n   2. Any issues or warnings encountered.\\n   3. Any manual steps the developer still needs to complete.\\n   4. Whether the project appears ready for development.\\n\\nDo not make assumptions beyond what is documented. Base your actions on the project's documentation whenever possible.\",\n.\\design\\activity\\project.json:387:          \"content\": \"I’ll inspect the repository’s setup instructions, package manifests, and current dependency state, then perform only the setup steps the project explicitly defines and verify the development checks that are available.\\n\\nThe repository defines three dependency sets: root tooling plus `app` and `desktop`. The documented `install:all` installs only the two subprojects, so I’m also checking the root tooling explicitly; telemetry `.env` files are optional and I will not create empty local copies.\\n\\nNode.js 22.16.0 is installed, but VS Code’s auto-attach debugger is injected into terminal Node processes and caused the first npm probe to stall. I’ll clear that injection only fo